# On Venus, which is longer: a day or a year?
subtitle: "Guess whether a day or a year lasts longer on Venus, then hear why it spins so slowly, and backwards, so that the Sun rises in the west."

## On Venus, which is longer: a day or a year?
text: {"font_size":34}
sources:
    - id: venus
      title: Venus
      authors: Wikipedia (from NASA planetary fact sheets)
      year: 2025
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Venus
      finding: Venus turns once in 243 Earth days (retrograde) and orbits the Sun in 224.7 days; its solar day is about 117 Earth days; its equator turns at about 6.5 km/h

    shape orbit dashed #8f887c shape circle x 310 y 410 radius 240 style.stroke_width 2 hidden true
    shape sun #d9a21b shape circle x 310 y 410 radius 66 style.fill #f2c94c hidden true
    text sun_l "Sun" x 310 y 410 font_size 32 hidden true
    shape venus #b8893a shape circle x 550 y 410 radius 38 style.fill #e8c27a hidden true
    path flag #b5482e points [[550,410],[550,470]] style.stroke_width 5 hidden true
    group venus_set members ["venus","flag"] hidden true
    text venus_l "Venus" x 550 y 330 font_size 32 hidden true
    text year_t "Year: 225 days" #2f6b8f cites ["venus"] x 790 y 500 font_size 44 hidden true
    text day_t "Day: 243 days" #b5482e cites ["venus"] x 790 y 570 font_size 44 hidden true
    text spin "↻ spins backwards" #b5482e x 790 y 410 font_size 38 hidden true
    text west "Sunrise: west" #b5482e x 790 y 330 font_size 38 hidden true

    card title "On Venus: day or year, which is longer?"

A: Here's a puzzle about our neighbour Venus. Like Earth, it spins on its axis, and it circles the Sun.
    draw sun sun_l orbit

B: So which is longer there, a day or a year? A year, surely. A day is just one spin.
    draw venus_set venus_l

    quiz "On Venus, which takes longer: one turn on its axis, or one trip round the Sun?" store v
        right: The day. Venus takes 243 Earth days to turn once, but only 225 to go round the Sun.
        wrong: Venus is the slowest spinner of all the planets.
        + The day
        * The year
        * About the same

    draw day_t year_t parallel true

    erase venus_l

A: Watch the red marker. Venus goes once round the Sun, and in that time it hasn't quite turned once.
    move venus_set path [[-8.2,62.1],[-32.2,120],[-70.3,169.7],[-120,207.8],[-177.9,231.8],[-240,240],[-302.1,231.8],[-360,207.8],[-409.7,169.7],[-447.8,120],[-471.8,62.1],[-480,0],[-471.8,-62.1],[-447.8,-120],[-409.7,-169.7],[-360,-207.8],[-302.1,-231.8],[-240,-240],[-177.9,-231.8],[-120,-207.8],[-70.3,-169.7],[-32.2,-120],[-8.2,-62.1],[0,0]] rotate -333 pivot.ref venus duration 6 easing linear

B: So it turns once in more than a year? That's lazy.
    highlight day_t

A: Very. Its ground turns at about six and a half kilometres an hour: walking pace.
    text walk "turns at 6.5 km/h" #2f6b8f cites ["venus"] x 790 y 410 font_size 38

    erase walk

A: And it spins backwards, the opposite way to Earth and almost every other planet.
    draw spin

B: Backwards? So the Sun comes up on the wrong side?
    draw west

A: It does: on Venus the Sun rises in the west and sets in the east.
    highlight west

A: Why backwards? No one knows for sure. Maybe a giant collision long ago, or the Sun's pull on its thick atmosphere.
    highlight venus

A: And because it spins backwards as it orbits, the two motions add up: from one sunrise to the next takes about a hundred and seventeen Earth days.
    text s117 "sunrise to sunrise: 117 d" #b5482e cites ["venus"] x 740 y 250 font_size 32

B: So a whole Venus year has only about two sunrises. I'd love the lie-in.
    highlight s117 effect circle
---
